"TPS6293x 3.8-V to 30-V, 2-A, 3-A Synchronous Buck Converters in a SOT583 Package datasheet (Rev. D) (https://www.ti.com/lit/ds/symlink/tps62933.pdf)\nciteturn63view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 51; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.ti.com/lit/ds/symlink/tps62933.pdf\",\"pattern\":\"RFBT\"}); Total lines: 2631\nL64@P0: TPS62933O operates in out of audio mode to avoid\nL65@P0: audible noise.\nL66@P0: Device Information\nL67@P0: Part Number Package(1) Body Size (NOM)\nL68@P0: TPS6293x SOT583 (8) 1.60 mm × 2.10 mm\nL69@P0: (1) For all available packages, see the orderable addendum at\nL70@P0: the end of the data sheet.\nL71@P0: L\nL72@P0: CBST\nL73@P0: COUT\nL74@P0: RFBT\nL75@P0: RFBB\nL76@P0: VIN\nL77@P0: GND\nL78@P0: EN\nL79@P0: SS\nL80@P0: BST\nL81@P0: SW\nL82@P0: FB\nL83@P0: RT\nL84@P0: CIN\nL85@P0: VIN\nL86@P0: VOUT\nL87@P0: VEN\nL88@P0: Simplified Schematic\nL89@P0: I-Load (A)\nL90@P0: Efficiency (%)\nL91@P0: 0.001 0.005 0.02 0.05 0.1 0.2 0.5 1 2 3\nL92@P0: 0\nL93@P0: 20\nL94@P0: 40\nL95@P0: 60\nL96@P0: 80\nL97@P0: 100\nL98@P0: VOUT=3.3V\nL99@P0: VOUT=5V\nL100@P0: VOUT=12V\nL1204@P18: of input supply current required to regulate the output voltage at small loads. This trades off very good light-load\nL1205@P18: efficiency for larger output voltage ripple and variable switching frequency.\nL1206@P18: 9.3.3 Voltage Reference\nL1207@P18: The internal reference voltage, VREF, is designed at 0.8 V (typical). The negative feedback system of converter\nL1208@P18: produces a precise ±2% feedback voltage, VFB, over full temperature by scaling the output of a temperature\u0002stable internal band-gap circuit.\nL1209@P18: 9.3.4 Output Voltage Setting\nL1210@P18: A precision 0.8-V reference voltage, VREF, is used to maintain a tightly regulated output voltage over the entire\nL1211@P18: operating temperature range. The output voltage is set by a resistor divider from the output voltage to the FB\nL1212@P18: pin. TI recommends using 1% tolerance resistors with a low temperature coefficient for the FB divider. Select the\nL1213@P18: bottom-side resistor, RFBB, for the desired divider current and use Equation 1 to calculate the top-side resistor,\nL1214@P18: RFBT. Lower RFBB increases the divider current and reduces efficiency at very light load. Larger RFBB makes the\nL1215@P18: FB voltage more susceptible to noise, so larger RFBB values require a more carefully designed feedback path on\nL1216@P18: the PCB. Setting RFBB = 10 kΩ and RFBT in the range of 10 kΩ to 300 kΩ is recommended for most applications.\nL1217@P18: The tolerance and temperature variation of the resistor dividers affect the output voltage regulation.\nL1218@P18: VOUT\nL1219@P18: FB\nL1220@P18: RFBT\nL1221@P18: RFBB\nL1222@P18: Figure 9-2. Output Voltage Setting\nL1223@P18: OUT REF\nL1224@P18: FBT FBB\nL1225@P18: REF\nL1226@P18: V - V\nL1227@P18: R = × R\nL1228@P18: V\nL1229@P18: (1)\nL1230@P18: where\nL1231@P18: • VREF is the 0.8 V (the internal reference voltage).\nL1232@P18: • RFBB is 10 kΩ (recommended).\nL1233@P18: 9.3.5 Switching Frequency Selection\nL1234@P18: The switching frequency is set by the condition of the RT input. The condition of this input is detected when the\nL1235@P18: device is first enabled. Once the converter is running, the switching frequency selection is fixed and cannot be\nL1236@P18: changed until the next power-on cycle or EN toggle. Table 9-1 shows the selection programming. In adjustable\nL1237@P18: frequency mode, the switching frequency can be set between 200 kHz and 2200 kHz by proper selection of RT\nL1238@P18: resistor. See Equation 2.\nL1239@P18: www.ti.com\nL1240@P18: TPS62933, TPS62932, TPS62933F, TPS62933P, TPS62933O\nL1241@P18: SLUSEA4D – JUNE 2021 – REVISED AUGUST 2022\nL1242@P18: Copyright © 2022 Texas Instruments Incorporated Submit Document Feedback 19\nL1243@P18-19: Product Folder Links: TPS62933 TPS62932 TPS62933F TPS62933P TPS62933O(2)\nL1244@P19: where\nL1245@P19: • RT is the value of RT timing resistor in kΩ.\nL1246@P19: • fSW is the switching frequency in kHz.\nL1247@P19: Table 9-1. RT Pin Resistor Settings\nL1248@P19: RT Pin Resistance Switching Frequency\nL1249@P19: Floating > 280 kΩ 500 kHz\nL1250@P19: GND < 1 kΩ 1200 kHz\nL1251@P19: RT to GND 8.9 kΩ to 111 kΩ 200 kHz to 2200 kHz\nL1252@P19: Figure 9-3 indicates the required resistor value for RT to set a desired switching frequency.\nL1253@P19: RT (kohm)\nL1254@P19: fSW (kHz)\nL1255@P19: 8 18 28 38 48 58 68 78 88 98 108 118\nL1256@P19: 0\nL1257@P19: 200\nL1258@P19: 400\nL1259@P19: 600\nL1260@P19: 800\nL1261@P19: 1000\nL1262@P19: 1200\nL1263@P19: 1400\nL1264@P19: 1600\nL1265@P19: 1800\nL1266@P19: 2000\nL1267@P19: 2200\nL1268@P19: Figure 9-3. Switching Frequency vs RT\nL1269@P19: There are four cases where the switching frequency does not conform to the condition set by the RT pin:\nL1270@P19: • Light load operation (PFM mode)\nL1271@P19: • Low dropout operation\nL1272@P19: • Minimum on-time operation\nL1273@P19: • Current limit tripped\nL1274@P19: Under all of these cases, the switching frequency folds back, meaning it is less than that programmed by the RT\nL1275@P19: pin. During these conditions, the output voltage remains in regulation, except for current limit operation.\nL1276@P19: 9.3.6 Enable and Adjusting Undervoltage Lockout\nL1277@P19: The EN pin provides electrical ON and OFF control of the device. When the EN pin voltage exceeds the enable\nL1278@P19: threshold voltage, VEN_RISE, the TPS6293x begins operation. If the EN pin voltage is pulled below the disable\nL1279@P19: threshold voltage, VEN_FALL, the converter stops switching and enters shutdown mode.\nL1280@P19: The EN pin has an internal pullup current source, which allows the user to float the EN pin to enable the device.\nL1946@P31: a ripple current rating greater than the maximum input current ripple. The input ripple current can be calculated\nL1947@P31: using Equation 22.\nL1948@P31: \u0010\nL1949@P31:  u u OUT IN_MIN OUT\nL1950@P31: CIN_RMS OUT\nL1951@P31: IN_MIN IN_MIN\nL1952@P31: V V V\nL1953@P31: I I\nL1954@P31: V V\nL1955@P31: (22)\nL1956@P31: For this example design, two TDK CGA5L1X7R1H106K160AC (10-μF, 50-V, 1206, X7R) capacitors have been\nL1957@P31: selected. The effective capacitance under input voltage of 24 V for each one is 3.45 μF. The input capacitance\nL1958@P31: value determines the input ripple voltage of the converter. The input voltage ripple can be calculated using\nL1959@P31: TPS62933, TPS62932, TPS62933F, TPS62933P, TPS62933O\nL1960@P31: SLUSEA4D – JUNE 2021 – REVISED AUGUST 2022 www.ti.com\nL1961@P31: 32 Submit Document Feedback Copyright © 2022 Texas Instruments Incorporated\nL1962@P31-32: Product Folder Links: TPS62933 TPS62932 TPS62933F TPS62933P TPS62933OEquation 23. Using the design example values, IOUT_MAX = 3 A, CIN_EFF = 2 × 3.45 = 6.9 μF, and fSW = 500 kHz,\nL1963@P32: yields an input voltage ripple of 222 mV and a RMS input ripple current of 1.22 A.\nL1964@P32: u\nL1965@P32: ' \u000e u\nL1966@P32: u\nL1967@P32: OUT _MAX\nL1968@P32: IN OUT _MAX ESR _MAX\nL1969@P32: IN SW\nL1970@P32: I 0.25\nL1971@P32: V (I R )\nL1972@P32: C f\nL1973@P32: (23)\nL1974@P32: where\nL1975@P32: • RESR_MAX is the maximum series resistance of the input capacitor, which is approximately 1.5 mΩ of two\nL1976@P32: capacitors in paralleled.\nL1977@P32: 10.2.2.10 Feedforward Capacitor CFF Selection\nL1978@P32: In some cases, a feedforward capacitor can be used across RFBT to improve the load transient response or\nL1979@P32: improve the loop phase margin. This is especially true when values of RFBT > 100 kΩ are used. Large values\nL1980@P32: of RFBT in combination with the parasitic capacitance at the FB pin can create a small signal pole that interferes\nL1981@P32: with the loop stability. A CFF helps mitigate this effect. Use lower values to determine if any advantage is gained\nL1982@P32: by the use of a CFF capacitor.\nL1983@P32: The Optimizing Transient Response of Internally Compensated DC-DC Converters with Feedforward Capacitor\nL1984@P32: Application Report is helpful when experimenting with a feedforward capacitor.\nL1985@P32: For this example design, a 10-pF capacitor C9 can be mounted to boost load transient performance.\nL1986@P32: 10.2.2.11 Maximum Ambient Temperature\nL1987@P32: As with any power conversion device, the TPS6293x dissipates internal power while operating. The effect of\nL1988@P32: this power dissipation is to raise the internal temperature of the converter above ambient. The internal die\nL1989@P32: temperature (TJ) is a function of the following:\nL1990@P32: • Ambient temperature\nL1991@P32: • Power loss\nL1992@P32: • Effective thermal resistance, RθJA, of the device\nL1993@P32: • PCB combination\nL1994@P32: The maximum internal die temperature must be limited to 150°C. This establishes a limit on the maximum\nL1995@P32: device power dissipation and, therefore, the load current. Equation 24 shows the relationships between the\nL1996@P32: important parameters. It is easy to see that larger ambient temperatures (TA) and larger values of RθJA reduce\nL1997@P32: the maximum available output current. The converter efficiency can be estimated by using the curves provided in\nL1998@P32: this data sheet. Note that these curves include the power loss in the inductor. If the desired operating conditions\nL1999@P32: cannot be found in one of the curves, then interpolation can be used to estimate the efficiency. Alternatively, the\nL2000@P32: EVM can be adjusted to match the desired application requirements and the efficiency can be measured directly.\nL2001@P32: The correct value of RθJA is more difficult to estimate. As stated in the Semiconductor and IC Package Thermal\nL2002@P32: Metrics Application Report, the value of RθJA given in the Thermal Information table is not valid for design\nL2003@P32: purposes and must not be used to estimate the thermal performance of the application. The values reported in\nL2004@P32: that table were measured under a specific set of conditions that are rarely obtained in an actual application. The\nL2005@P32: data given for RθJC(bott) and ΨJT can be useful when determining thermal performance. See the Semiconductor\nL2006@P32: and IC Package Thermal Metrics Application Report for more information and the resources given at the end of\nL2007@P32: this section.\nL2008@P32: 1\nL2009@P32: 1 T\nL2010@P32: K\nL2011@P32:  u u\nL2012@P32: \u0010 K\nL2013@P32: J A\nL2014@P32: OUT _MAX\nL2015@P32: JA OUT\nL2016@P32: (T -T ) I\nL2017@P32: R V\nL2018@P32: (24)\nL2019@P32: where\nL2020@P32: • ŋ is efficiency.\nL2021@P32: The effective RθJA is a critical parameter and depends on many factors such as the following:\nL2022@P32: • Power dissipation\nL2023@P32: www.ti.com\nL2024@P32: TPS62933, TPS62932, TPS62933F, TPS62933P, TPS62933O\nL2025@P32: SLUSEA4D – JUNE 2021 – REVISED AUGUST 2022\nL2026@P32: Copyright © 2022 Texas Instruments Incorporated Submit Document Feedback 33\nL2027@P32-33: Product Folder Links: TPS62933 TPS62932 TPS62933F TPS62933P TPS62933O• Air temperature and flow\nL2028@P33: • PCB area\nL2029@P33: • Copper heat-sink area\nL2030@P33: • Number of thermal vias under the package\nL2031@P33: • Adjacent component placement\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTPS6293x 3.8-V to 30-V, 2-A, 3-A Synchronous Buck Converters in a SOT583 Package datasheet (Rev. D) (https://www.ti.com/lit/ds/symlink/tps62933.pdf)\nciteturn63view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 51; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.ti.com/lit/ds/symlink/tps62933.pdf\",\"pattern\":\"SLUSE\"}); Total lines: 2631\nNo matching text found for \"SLUSE\"--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nXAL50xx Shielded Power Inductors (https://www.coilcraft.com/getmedia/49bc46c8-4b2c-45b9-9b6c-2eaa235ea698/xal50xx.pdf)\nciteturn63view2 [w